<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article article-type="other" xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2212139</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Cover</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title/></title-group><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>16</day><month>2</month><year>1998</year></pub-date><volume>187</volume><issue>4</issue><fpage>cover</fpage><lpage>cover</lpage><copyright-year>1998</copyright-year></article-meta></front><body><p>Plasma cells are abnormally located within the periarteriolar lymphoid sheaths (T cell area) of mouse spleen in TNF-R1&#x02013;deficient mice and this phenotype is not restored after bone marrow reconstitution. The red fluorescence indicates IgM-positive cells and the  green fluorescence reveals the Thy1-positive T cells. The bright red labeled&#x02013;cells within the green areas are plasma cells. See related article in  this issue by Tkachuk et al., <related-article related-article-type="article-reference" vol="187" page="469" id="N0x1b73610N0x1e564e0" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="9463397" ext-link-type="pubmed">pp. 469&#x02013;477</related-article>.</p></body></article>